  CONTEXT's op run, which may be many levels up -- so the ops must TRAVEL,
  not be reordered where produced. They travel as a :stack push/pop span in
  the op vector, carrying no :x/:y so translate-ops and transform-ops leave
  them alone; the first ancestor that is a context extracts every span it
  can see, sorts by level with ties in document order, and splices
  negatives after its own :node op and the rest at the end. Riding in the
  op vector rather than a new return key kept this entirely out of
  layout-flex, layout-grid, layout-table, layout-multicol and
  layout-inline-run -- each already concatenates its children's :draw.
  Extraction FLATTENS (a span inside a span becomes its sibling) because a
  z-index:auto positioned box is not a context; measured through two nested
  wrappers. layout-absolute-children STOPPED sorting: a local sort by :z
  cannot be right, since the containing block is not the stacking context."
